<PageTitle>Privacy and room data — Interlock Expeditions</PageTitle>
<article class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Actual data boundaries</p><h1>Rooms are shared. Keys are private.</h1><p>The service stores host aliases, hashed recovery credentials and sessions; room titles, authored episode snapshots and clocks; guest aliases, crew roles and activity times; and accepted machine actions, their fingerprints and room event summaries. It does not request a camera, microphone, location or contact list.</p><h2>Who can read a room?</h2><p>Its host account can manage the room and see the roster and progress. A guest needs its own private room-player authority to read a crew card or change that crew's controls. Another host account cannot read your control page. A room invitation allows joining subject to its code and capacity; it is not a host or existing player's private credential.</p><h2>On this device</h2><p>The host session is in a secure HttpOnly cookie. Guests also have a private cookie; a separate per-room recovery capability is stored in tab storage and device storage to survive reconnects and separate tab sessions. Sharing an invitation copies neither credential. Device storage is a convenience, while actual multiplayer progress is in the server database.</p><h2>Retention and erasure</h2><p>Completed rooms remain until their host erases them. A host can end and delete a room, removing its player records, progress, reports and event rows from the active database. Deleting a host account removes its rooms and sessions. Hosts have a limit of fifty retained rooms. Hosting and recovery infrastructure may maintain its own operational copies; this interface does not claim to erase independent provider backups.</p><h2>Diagnostics and reports</h2><p>Service logs record action types, statuses and timing without raw credentials or submitted machine bodies. A rate-limit identity is derived from the hosting-provided address with a private salt. Room reports store the selected reason and the text you explicitly submit; do not paste recovery keys or unrelated personal information. A report does not automatically remove another player's account.</p><h2>Payment and advertising</h2><p>No payment or advertising is fabricated. Paid allowances require configured providers and verified provider state. When advertising is not configured, its script is not loaded. Private crew and host screens are not advertising placements. Provider configuration and actual availability are shown separately from any future business hypothesis.</p><p>The service is hosted on Cloudflare; its source is published on GitHub. Published fictional stories and solutions are public source material, which is different from access to real room records.</p></article>
